  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front is stalled to the south of ENC with \nlight N'rly flow bringing dry and \"relatively\" cooler air over \nthe region this afternoon. Highs have gotten into the 80s today \nthough it is less humid than previous days.\n\nAs we get into tonight, winds look to once again decouple under\nmo clear skies as high pressure centered to the north slides \nE'wards towards the Eastern Seaboard. Low level moisture remains \nin place across portions of ENC especially across areas that saw\nrain on Monday. Given this, would not rule out the development \nof patchy fog or low stratus tonight after about midnight. \nLatest sounding data for this evening does look supportive for \nfog/low stratus development given a high potential for a low \nlevel inversion to develop thus trapping low level moisture in \nplace. Will note there is one thing working against fog/low \nstratus development this evening which would be the continued \nadvection of dry air today. For now to account for the \nuncertainty have capped visibility to 5 miles mainly along the\nCrystal Coast and left patchy fog in areas I have the highest \nconfidence in for now. Further adjustments either up or down \nwill likely be necessary to visibility tonight depending on \ntrends. Otherwise lows tonight get into the upper 60s to low \n70s. On Wednesday, surface flow becomes more onshore, kicking \noff a period of increasing moisture and instability across the \nSoutheast U.S. coastline with a modest uptick of shower and \nthunderstorm coverage as a wave of low pressure lifts a weak \nfront northwards towards ENC.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another wave of low pressure is forecast to \nlift along the same front Thursday into Friday, pulling it \nfurther north into ENC and providing a better focal point for \nmore widespread coverage of showers and storms ahead of a second\nfront which will cross the area by Sat. Given the multiple \ndays of onshore flow, PWATs do return back closer to 2\" from \nThurs onwards and with enough instability in place but a lack of\nshear, a few isolated stronger storms aren't completely out of \nthe question though not currently anticipated. In addition to \nthis, any shower or thunderstorm that develops will be an \nefficient rainmaker bringing torrential rainfall and a low end \nrisk for flash flooding, mainly in urban and poor drainage \nareas. High pressure builds back in behind Sat's front on Sun \nbringing a return back to more climatological precip chances \nalong the seabreeze. Temps into next week remain seasonable with\nhighs in the 80s and lows in the mid 60s to mid 70s.",
